<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # One, Two, Three Bananas! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Go Shopping!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> One, Two, Three Bananas!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Talk about everyday objects and how they are used. Interactive: Express preferences about familiar topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can talk about everyday objects at the store and simply explain how they are used (e.g., "I use a basket to carry food."). Can express preferences for shopping items (e.g., "I like apples."). Can describe basic items at the store using simple words and phrases (e.g., "Apples are expensive.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> "Fruit Basket Chant and Say". The teacher displays large flashcards of fruits (pineapple, apple, orange, banana) and numbers (one to five, then ten). The teacher points to a flashcard (e.g., three apples) and chants: "One, two, three, apples! Three apples!" Students repeat chorally. The teacher repeats with different fruits and numbers, encouraging clear pronunciation and counting.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of three bananas. The teacher clearly and slowly says, "I want three bananas, please." The teacher then holds up a flashcard of two pineapples and says, "Can I have two pineapples, please?" The teacher emphasizes the polite expressions "I want," "Can I have," and "please," along with the pronunciation of /b/ in "banana" and /p/ in "pineapple."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "When I want fruit, do I say 'I want'?" (Students respond: Yes, and teacher models 'Yes' gesture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Do I say 'thank you' or 'please' when I ask?" (Students respond: Please, and teacher models polite gesture).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue displayed on the board or as a handout, featuring two characters (e.g., Ana and Mr. Smith, a customer and a cashier) at a fruit stand. The dialogue uses previously introduced shopping vocabulary (pineapple, apple, orange, banana, price, expensive, buy, sell, money) and focuses on requesting items and expressing quantity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br>
                Ana: "Hello, Mr. Smith! Can I have three bananas, please?"<br>
                Mr. Smith: "Here you go, Ana. Three fresh bananas."<br>
                Ana: "Thank you! How much are the pineapples?"<br>
                Mr. Smith: "Pineapples are ten dollars each. They are a little expensive today."<br>
                Ana: "Oh. I want one apple, please."<br>
                Mr. Smith: "One apple for you. That's eight dollars for the apple and bananas."<br>
                Ana: "Here is my money. Thank you, bye!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reads the dialogue aloud, using gestures and intonation, and then has students listen and repeat chorally and individually, focusing on pronunciation of /b/ and /p/ and blending of phonemes for 'money'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing quantity, requesting items politely, asking about price, and describing items using simple adjectives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (oral questions):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Who wants bananas?" (Ana)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "How many apples does Ana want?" (One)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Are the pineapples cheap or expensive?" (Expensive)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using "Number + noun" (e.g., "three bananas"), Present Simple for polite requests ("I want...", "Can I have..."), and shopping vocabulary (price, buy, sell, fresh, expensive). Practicing pronunciation of /b/ in "banana" and /p/ in "pineapple", and blending for numbers like "six" and word "money".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice/cloze:</b> "Shopping Sentence Builders". The teacher displays sentence frames on the board/projector: "I want ______ (number) ______ (fruit), please." and "Can I have ______ (number) ______ (fruit), please?" Students take turns orally filling in the blanks using flashcards displayed (e.g., the teacher points to "four" and "oranges," and students say: "I want four oranges, please."). The teacher actively corrects pronunciation of /b/ in 'banana' and /p/ in 'pineapple' as students speak.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled substitution drill:</b> "Fruit Request Chain". Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by saying, "I want two apples, please." The next student repeats the previous sentence and adds a new request with a different fruit and number, e.g., "I want two apples, please, and I want three bananas, please." The activity continues around the circle, focusing on accurate use of "I want," "Can I have," numbers (one, two, three, four, five, ten), and fruit names.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction/peer check:</b> "Polite Shopping Match". Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of flashcards with numbers (one, two, three, four, five, ten) and fruit pictures (pineapple, apple, orange, banana). Student A picks a number and a fruit card and says, "Can I have [number] [fruit], please?" Student B responds, "Yes, here you go," or "No, I don't have [fruit]." Then they switch roles. Partners provide simple peer feedback on clear speech and polite expressions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Market Role-play – "My Fruit Stand".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group sets up a "fruit stand" using flashcards, drawings, or even real fruit (if available) for different fruits with various quantities. One student acts as the "vendor" (cashier/seller), and the others are "customers".</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Customers approach the stand and politely request items using "I want [number] [fruit], please" or "Can I have [number] [fruit], please?". They can also ask about the "price" ("How much is the [fruit]?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The vendor practices responding, "Here you go!" or "Sorry, I don't have that," or stating a simple "price" ("It's two dollars.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns being the vendor, ensuring everyone has a chance to practice both roles.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, listening for the fluent use of target grammar, vocabulary, pronunciation (especially /b/ and /p/, and phoneme blending for words like 'money'), and appropriate polite expressions in a communicative context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Fruit Stand" role-play activity, focusing on individual student performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the market role-play in their groups, demonstrating their ability to request items and express quantity politely.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clearly pronounces fruit names, numbers, and specifically /b/ in "banana" and /p/ in "pineapple." Blends phonemes for words like "six" and "money."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Uses at least 5 different shopping-related nouns, verbs, or adjectives (e.g., apple, buy, expensive, money, basket) during the interaction.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and quantity:</b> Accurately uses "Number + noun" (e.g., "three bananas") and "Present simple" for requests ("I want...", "Can I have...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and politeness:</b> Interacts appropriately with peers, using polite expressions (e.g., "please", "thank you") in context.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (oral or quick written check):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can ask for fruit politely: Yes / A little / No</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can say how many fruits I want: Yes / A little / No</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (oral or quick written response):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite fruit to ask for today? Why?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to say in English today? What was a little difficult?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The "My Fruit Stand" role-play activity worked very well for most students to practice requesting items and expressing quantity. The use of flashcards and realia helped visual learners actively engage. Some students needed more prompting for consistent use of polite expressions like "please" and "thank you." Pronunciation of /p/ in "pineapple" and blending phonemes for "money" was challenging for a few, requiring more individual modeling and correction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">For the next lesson, I will provide more explicit sentence starters for polite requests and incorporate a quick, targeted pronunciation drill at the beginning. I will also ensure groups are balanced to encourage peer support for quieter students during interactive tasks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will consolidate their understanding of shopping vocabulary and sentence structures by writing simple sentences and short descriptive paragraphs about items they want to buy, preparing for a shopping list activity. They will use the polite phrases practiced today in their written requests.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>